  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nUpper level ridging centered over the central and southern US with \naxis stretched northwestward toward the Northern Rockies will \ngenerally be the pattern that persists through much of the upcoming \nweek across the region. The ridge is in its initial stages of \nrebuilding, with a drier zonal flow aloft still persisting across \nthe region. This overall drier setup, along with cooler surface \ntemperatures behind a cold front will inhibit shower or thunderstorm \ndevelopment through the remainder of the day and into tonight.\nWildfire smoke will be around at times through much of the \nupcoming week.\n\nMonsoonal moisture will begin to move northward through the western \nperiphery of this upper level ridging as a more southerly or \nsouthwesterly flow aloft develops Tuesday onward. Afternoon and \nevening showers and thunderstorms initially look to remain confined \nto areas across Southwest Montana before spreading further north \nWednesday and Thursday. Anomalous moisture associated with the \nmonsoonal push combining with weak winds aloft look to result in \nslower storm motions by Wednesday, particularly across Southwest \nMontana. This will yield an increased risk for excessive rainfall \nwhich leads to flash flooding. Those in urban areas or near burn \nscars or steep terrain should be aware of this increased risk for \nexcessive rainfall Wednesday. \n\nStorm motions look to increase slightly for Thursday, though still \nremain on the slower side. At this point confidence is highest in \nWednesday being the day with the greatest risk for excessive \nrainfall, with Southwest Montana being the area of greatest concern. \n\nThe risk for severe weather Wednesday and Thursday looks to be more \nfavorable further north on the plains, where forecast soundings show \nslightly deeper inverted-V soundings. The lack of a stronger forcing \nmechanism raises concerns as to how widespread shower and \nthunderstorm development will be on the plains, but should it occur \nthe risk for strong wind gusts would certainly exist. \n\nBy Friday the most robust monsoonal moisture begins to be shunted \nfurther south and east again as a drier zonal flow develops \nacross the region. Lingering moisture looks sufficient to result \nin another round of afternoon showers and thunderstorms Friday, \nthough coverage looks lower at this point and the risk for \nexcessive rainfall looks to decrease significantly. What this does\nmean however is that gusty westerly winds will begin to work back\nas temperatures continue to trend hotter Friday. As of now the \nbetter monsoonal moisture looks to remain south of the region this\nweekend, which would keep things on the hotter and drier side. \nThose with outdoor plans this weekend should plan for plenty of \nbreaks and to hydrate often to help mitigate concerns from the \nheat. Areas that see little precipitation this week will have \nincreased concern for fire weather this weekend given forecast\nhot, dry, and breezy conditions. -AM\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nThe main source of uncertainty at this point looks to occur on \nWednesday and Thursday, associated with the risk for excessive \nrainfall in southern areas and potential for strong to severe \nthunderstorms in northern areas. Where this transition in threat \noccurs will largely be dictated by where the 'strongest' flow \naloft resides. Should this stronger flow aloft trend northward, \nthe risk for excessive rainfall leading to flash flooding would \nedge northward and vice versa. It should be noted that excessive \nrainfall leading to flash flooding will be a concern in urban \nareas and burn scars, regardless of where the strongest flow aloft\nends up settling. -AM",
